COORDINATOR REDIRECT (this round): the ladder hunt above found the naive
resolution ladder's solvable/certified-grid set sparse -- rungs 1-2 do not
even converge at fixed alpha=-0.3447 (floor at 6.5e-3 to 7.1e-3, alpha
implied by the gauge solution visibly drifting off -0.3447 as Newton
proceeds -- a genuine "no root at this fixed alpha for this grid" outcome,
consistent with the campaign's own corner-dust/panel-sensitivity lore: the
solvable-grid set under a FIXED-alpha Newton solve is sparse, not dense,
between the gate grid and production scale), and rungs 3-4 DO converge but
cholesky(P) FAILS both times (4 negative eigenvalues of 4102 and of 5206
respectively) -- Hurwitz-ness on ker(Cg) has NOT turned on anywhere in this
candidate ladder, all the way up to n_f=5208 (LARGER than the production
root's own n_f=4760).

Rather than keep ascending an unbounded ladder, this run instead reuses the
ALREADY cholesky-certified PRODUCTION root (pnorm_P.npz, root A: degs=
(16,40,12) Nb=36 eps_b=1e-4, n_f=4760, NOTE_CLAIMS S4 / PNORM_PROBE.out) and
makes the march there affordable by FREEZING the reduced Jacobian (see
run_production_pnorm_gate / QuasiNewtonSMarcher in march_s.py) instead of
paying SMarcher._reduced_correction's per-Newton-iteration dense rebuild
(the cost that made rungs 3/4's Lyapunov builds alone take 525s/1182s
above, and would make a full march at that scale far more expensive
still). The frozen-Jacobian scheme was verified (not merely assumed) to
reproduce SMarcher's exact per-step result to 4.6e-14 relative difference
on the gate grid before being trusted at production scale -- see the
module docstring section above QuasiNewtonSMarcher in march_s.py for the
closed-form identity (project_oblique is the identity on ker(Cg)) that
makes this exact on the reduced block, not just a convenient
approximation.
================================================================================

